#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176749} :activity/id #uuid "b7c1b63f-a8ec-54b2-8bd2-f993ffba0210" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049553 :topic/id #uuid "fed8aeb7-cb56-5ca6-9352-ed88fd02f45e" :topic/math-academy-id 3636} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177001 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to construct the marginal mass functions for two discrete random variables from their joint probability mass function. We now wish to formulate analogous definitions when our random variables are continuous. \n\nFirst, recall that if $f(x,y)$ is the joint PMF of the *discrete* random variables $X$ and $Y$ with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y$, respectively, then\n\n- the marginal mass function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_X(x)  = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{y \\in S_Y} f(x,y)\n$$\n\n- and the marginal mass function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_Y(y)  = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S_X} f(x,y).\n$$\n\nNow suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are *continuous* random variables with joint probability density function $f(x, y).$  \n\n- The **marginal density function** of $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_X(x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\n- The **marginal density function** of $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_Y(y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f (x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe sometimes refer to a marginal density function as a **marginal probability density function** (or **marginal PDF**).\n\nIt's helpful to appreciate the analogy between the discrete and continuous cases as this often helps us to remember their definitions. In summary:\n\n- To convert from discrete to continuous, we turn sums into integrals.\n\n- When we compute $f_X$ in the discrete case, we sum over all possible values of $Y.$ In the continuous case, we integrate with respect to $y.$\n\n- When we compute $f_Y$ in the discrete case, we sum over all possible values of $X.$ In the continuous case, we integrate with respect to $x.$\n\nLet's now see a concrete example of how to compute a marginal density function." :tutorial/id #uuid "c0ae531f-9bb0-5ba9-bd88-40955c3b086f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5478 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5ee40682-9346-5226-8f31-7485c414635e" :step/math-academy-id 34136 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176843}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176755 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175999 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175852 :question/id #uuid "ed593b5a-be86-5f9a-b9a5-d82b07ed2cfd" :question/math-academy-id "e-9192"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2b5bc-ecf8-5e8e-bf4e-0cde666ec594" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049728}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476940}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal Distribution From a Joint Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "e1c872df-df0e-5ace-95d4-79d04118e90c" :step/math-academy-id 34284 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176756}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176756 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176000 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175853 :question/id #uuid "e0123816-b3f6-5fd4-a6ae-09c31bcce5cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-9193"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc918d1c-7446-59a5-ad73-f705f83ae49c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507417}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal Probability From a Joint Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "e005bf1f-d37f-59a9-8616-8c44dcccf27f" :step/math-academy-id 34287 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176001 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175854 :question/id #uuid "acab7d19-e027-53cc-b491-58cbd73ee640" :question/math-academy-id "e-9195"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad789c4f-c4e1-5efc-8241-a55e4ac1dd07" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050750}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476948}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal CDF"} :step/id #uuid "92b212c1-f8b5-5c20-9ba2-51519f5eb1c8" :step/math-academy-id 34294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176037 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175684 :question/id #uuid "b26c36dd-e371-5598-96a3-73fb1c147a7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10048"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc2759a6-76b0-5410-b695-1e28b3168cb8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507488}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal Distribution From a Joint Distribution: Non-Rectangular Domains"} :step/id #uuid "79e0c173-47c4-57c7-89d0-44529d8a86f6" :step/math-academy-id 37919 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176844}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176843 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177056 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n![](images/e2/e229c78e51036e7fb7b7e9345bbe4f2ede5704032d65d2581b53c8a5f59ef603.svg)\n\nLet's find the expression for the marginal density function $f_X(x)$ of $X.$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\leq x \\leq 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[{x}^{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}]-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot [0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for $f_X(x)$ is given by\n\n![](images/e8/e8eb5c2dedf51c1b232af9e2dd6a4eb12621daaa78ab34f18a46e26a9a6d2887.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "3374b507-4cf9-507d-a469-7279c8a65dde" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6292 :tutorial/title "A Concrete Example"} :step/id #uuid "1d45cbe3-5cd1-5dd4-b04c-1c3e4790d561" :step/math-academy-id 38107 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176755}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177057 :tutorial/content "Recall that the cumulative distribution function (or CDF) of a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X\\leq x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nNow suppose we know the marginal distributions $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ of the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y.$\n\nThen we can define the **marginal cumulative distribution functions** (or **marginal CDFs**) of $X$ and $Y$ analogously as follows:\n\n- The marginal CDF of $X,$ denoted $F_X(x),$ is given by\n\n$$\nF_X(x) = P(X\\leq x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x f_X(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\n- The marginal CDF of $Y,$ denoted $F_Y(y),$ is given by\n\n$$\nF_Y(y) = P(Y\\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y f_Y(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "cf432491-2aee-56d2-9488-c13965e4b0c7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6293 :tutorial/title "Marginal Cumulative Distribution Functions"} :step/id #uuid "f9edd5a3-d60c-5e03-ab11-6e9add487f22" :step/math-academy-id 38110 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176757}}] :activity/title "Marginal Distributions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176153}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507535} :activity/id #uuid "61f74d27-8967-5278-99ab-939f2c9f5d63" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049553 :topic/id #uuid "fed8aeb7-cb56-5ca6-9352-ed88fd02f45e" :topic/math-academy-id 3636} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507535 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176262 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5559f4b-1521-523b-8406-0bc5d66dde15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173833} {:answer/id #uuid "0256d74d-a236-57b6-be17-032aa4d685c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173834} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4e18a3-88e7-5f78-901b-41cc43b03702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506903} {:answer/id #uuid "8b6fd457-4eb9-5f91-a02c-38afa9b1bc0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506988} {:answer/id #uuid "eb17bacf-1aea-52d5-8089-fcb79fd9e5c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507198}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506903} :answer-field/id #uuid "d58894b2-ff7d-514e-beee-5c2f7fe8ec3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d2b8ae16-dd41-57f5-a30f-634ebab68387" :question/math-academy-id "q-110422" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4xy, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ g(x), & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4x{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =2x[{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =2x({1}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}{f}_{X}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}2u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-0 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{X}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(x)={x}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "0ffd72bb-a8c0-5855-b23b-6732826ff26e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507544}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507544 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ea94da0-212f-53c3-a486-393d1299b039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-4{x}^{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506906} {:answer/id #uuid "7fdfe9ca-4b60-5702-9714-ab58418f727d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6{x}^{5}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506960} {:answer/id #uuid "a54fc47c-9a0e-57b6-8b89-be9050bd877c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6{x}^{2}-5{x}^{4}}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507037} {:answer/id #uuid "eea6aa59-5b2f-56da-964c-d354c3437006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-12{x}^{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507204} {:answer/id #uuid "f64e3710-d943-541a-b12e-b9944246e6ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507221}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507221} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e637269-5e6b-5cf4-8954-d277add9508e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bc9a9ffe-ea0b-5f0e-a6de-e484fad54774" :question/math-academy-id "q-142703" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with a joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=15{x}^{2}y,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{X}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}.$\n\n![](images/df/dfa15482cfa0e8fdc9e98b05a71d2b64a89b3886da44f6966fee9f1b5e096eef.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}15{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =15{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =15{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}[{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}[{x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}})}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}[{x}^{2}\\cdot (1-{x}^{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{15({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "31d77338-adef-522f-8da7-bc7e285aec17" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507554}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507428 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49ad0885-f8da-524f-9005-e5e1b037ad4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y-2{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506853} {:answer/id #uuid "7115d60d-e9d8-5f83-a19f-079b5ac98a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2}, & 0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506938} {:answer/id #uuid "7e391690-8700-5883-ae9c-3a383d2225f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4}, & 0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506955} {:answer/id #uuid "a0cf1e33-8ff7-5254-a612-ffb05d938e3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6y-3{y}^{2}}{4}, & 0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507023} {:answer/id #uuid "e1bf8a30-3690-5c0d-8f4e-7c1805175b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4}, & 0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507171}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507171} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0102b4d-840b-5f84-bda9-896fcec58961" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "43cc234d-7098-5a99-82b7-39099388a118" :question/math-academy-id "q-142707" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with a joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{2},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\}.$\n\n![](images/f6/f65ef4fc9d7ae895bc7ba8fe99bc7b03d8b26c92611fcbc43f600aa2e0c30301.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\frac{xy}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[{x}^{2}y]_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[{(\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}})}^{2}\\cdot y-{0}^{2}\\cdot y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4-{y}^{2})y-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[4y-{y}^{3}] \\\\  & =y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4}, & 0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5ea203c3-b8b6-5671-9cd4-e1bd1eb943ad" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507565}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507565 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507417 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20316b3d-871f-52f5-b903-85bdc45f6d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3536" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506741} {:answer/id #uuid "677bc76f-78b2-58a6-be32-39f8b6cf5041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506921} {:answer/id #uuid "aa3dbd41-a77d-56c3-871d-26a1c27b3178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507053} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a695a9-e856-5582-be47-9f7aea992d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8348" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507189} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ba3ad6-e42c-5fa6-bd84-8efbfdfdfc34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1768" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507225}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506741} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b0c6ed0-18fa-583a-b1bb-5250eadea4f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3631ecfc-d4a3-5620-abd5-3d7860073884" :question/math-academy-id "q-142908" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{x}\\,y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,2\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X<0.5).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{x}\\,y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{10}\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2}]}_{y=2}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\sqrt{x}({3}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<0.5) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0.5}{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{0.5} \\\\  & =[{0.5}^{3/2}]-[{0}^{3/2}] \\\\  & ≈0.3536.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8e3d3759-ee1e-5e5e-ada8-644f3c813177" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507586}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013c2556-2676-5788-87c5-ed7f14fbe60e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506680} {:answer/id #uuid "3376b66e-72f1-521c-9aa1-68703ead3d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506803} {:answer/id #uuid "58549a00-2751-552c-af49-cc906a42a61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506881} {:answer/id #uuid "63a2b90c-fbfd-585e-af5d-498e3093f055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506914} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b57d6d-be30-5d75-a19b-cec00a3bf106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507170}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506881} :answer-field/id #uuid "00986474-0f08-5b7b-8afa-1dc60c06bfa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0b0ce4fe-46e8-561a-bdf9-b2690fdeeb0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-142909" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{19}x\\sqrt{y}, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,4\\le y\\le 9, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\ge 1.9).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{4}^{9}\\frac{1}{19}x\\sqrt{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{19}x{[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3/2}]}_{y=4}^{y=9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{57}x{[{y}^{3/2}]}_{y=4}^{y=9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{57}x({9}^{3/2}-{4}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}x, & 1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 1.9) & ={\\int }_{1.9}^{\\infty }{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1.9}^{2}\\frac{2}{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{1.9}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{2}^{2}-{1.9}^{2}] \\\\  & =0.13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e3721229-b361-5d7c-96cf-50883a304a69"}] :activity/title "Marginal Distributions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506669}]}
